⟦P0⟧Tutorial: Create a Go module⟦P1⟧This is the first part of a tutorial that introduces a few fundamental⟦P2⟧features of the Go language. If you're just getting started with Go, be sure⟦P3⟧to take a look at⟦P4⟧⟦P5⟧Tutorial: Get started with Go⟦P6⟧, which introduces⟦P7⟧the ⟦P8⟧ command, Go modules, and very simple Go code.⟦P9⟧In this tutorial you'll create two modules. The first is a library which is⟦P10⟧intended to be imported by other libraries or applications. The second is a⟦P11⟧caller application which will use the first.⟦P12⟧This tutorial's sequence includes seven brief topics that each illustrate a⟦P13⟧different part of the language.⟦P14⟧Create a module ⟦P15⟧ Write a small module with functions you can call from⟦P16⟧another module.⟦P17⟧⟦P18⟧Call your code from another module⟦P19⟧ ⟦P20⟧⟦P21⟧Import and use your new module.⟦P22⟧⟦P23⟧Return and handle an error⟦P24⟧ ⟦P25⟧ Add simple⟦P26⟧error handling.⟦P27⟧⟦P28⟧Return a random greeting⟦P29⟧ ⟦P30⟧ Handle data⟦P31⟧in slices (Go's dynamically-sized arrays).⟦P32⟧⟦P33⟧Return greetings for multiple people⟦P34⟧⟦P35⟧⟦P36⟧ Store ⟦P37⟧ pairs in a map.⟦P38⟧⟦P39⟧Add a test⟦P40⟧ ⟦P41⟧ Use Go's built-in unit testing⟦P42⟧features to test your code.⟦P43⟧⟦P44⟧Compile and install the application⟦P45⟧ ⟦P46⟧⟦P47⟧Compile and install your code locally.⟦P48⟧⟦P49⟧Note:⟦P50⟧ For other tutorials, see⟦P51⟧⟦P52⟧Tutorials⟦P53⟧.⟦P54⟧Prerequisites⟦P55⟧⟦P56⟧Some programming experience.⟦P57⟧ The code here is pretty⟦P58⟧simple, but it helps to know something about functions, loops, and arrays.⟦P59⟧⟦P60⟧Go.⟦P61⟧ We recommend using the latest version of Go to follow this tutorial.⟦P62⟧For installation instructions, see ⟦P63⟧Installing Go⟦P64⟧.⟦P65⟧⟦P66⟧A tool to edit your code.⟦P67⟧ Any text editor you have will⟦P68⟧work fine. Most text editors have good support for Go. The most popular are⟦P69⟧VSCode (free), ⟦P70⟧ (paid), and Vim (free).⟦P71⟧⟦P72⟧A command terminal.⟦P73⟧ Go works well using any terminal on⟦P74⟧Linux and Mac, and on ⟦P75⟧ or cmd in Windows.⟦P76⟧Start a module that others can use⟦P77⟧Start by creating a Go module. In a⟦P78⟧module, you collect one or more related packages for a discrete and useful set⟦P79⟧of functions. For example, you might create a module with packages that have⟦P80⟧functions for doing financial analysis so that others writing financial⟦P81⟧applications can use your work. For more about developing modules, see⟦P82⟧⟦P83⟧Developing and publishing modules⟦P84⟧.⟦P85⟧Go code is grouped into packages, and packages are grouped into modules. Your⟦P86⟧module specifies dependencies needed to run your code, including the Go⟦P87⟧version and the set of other modules it requires.⟦P88⟧As you add or improve functionality in your module, you publish new versions⟦P89⟧of the module. Developers writing code that calls functions in your module can⟦P90⟧import the module's updated packages and test with the new version before⟦P91⟧putting it into production use.⟦P92⟧Open a command prompt and ⟦P93⟧ to your home directory.⟦P94⟧On Linux or Mac:⟦P95⟧On Windows:⟦P96⟧Create a ⟦P97⟧ directory for your Go module source code.⟦P98⟧For example, from your home directory use the following commands:⟦P99⟧Start your module using the⟦P100⟧⟦P101⟧⟦P102⟧ command⟦P103⟧.⟦P104⟧Run the ⟦P105⟧ command, giving it your module path ⟦P106⟧⟦P107⟧here, use ⟦P108⟧. If you publish a module,⟦P109⟧this ⟦P110⟧must⟦P111⟧ be a path from which your module can be downloaded by⟦P112⟧Go tools. That would be your code's repository.⟦P113⟧For more on naming your module with a module path, see⟦P114⟧⟦P115⟧Managing⟦P116⟧dependencies⟦P117⟧.⟦P118⟧The ⟦P119⟧ command creates a ⟦P120⟧ file to track your⟦P121⟧code's dependencies. So far, the file includes only the name of your⟦P122⟧module and the Go version your code supports. But as you add dependencies,⟦P123⟧the ⟦P124⟧ file will list the versions your code depends on. This keeps⟦P125⟧builds reproducible and gives you direct control over which module⟦P126⟧versions to use.⟦P127⟧In your text editor, create a file in which to write your code and call it⟦P128⟧⟦P129⟧.⟦P130⟧Paste the following code into your ⟦P131⟧ file and save the file.⟦P132⟧This is the first code for your module. It returns a greeting to any⟦P133⟧caller that asks for one. You'll write code that calls this function in⟦P134⟧the next step.⟦P135⟧In this code, you:⟦P136⟧Declare a ⟦P137⟧ package to collect related functions.⟦P138⟧Implement a ⟦P139⟧ function to return the greeting.⟦P140⟧This function takes a ⟦P141⟧ parameter whose type is⟦P142⟧⟦P143⟧. The function also returns a ⟦P144⟧.⟦P145⟧In Go, a function whose name starts with a capital letter can be⟦P146⟧called by a function not in the same package. This is known in Go as⟦P147⟧an exported name. For more about exported names, see⟦P148⟧⟦P149⟧Exported names⟦P150⟧ in the⟦P151⟧Go tour.⟦P152⟧Declare a ⟦P153⟧ variable to hold your greeting.⟦P154⟧In Go, the ⟦P155⟧ operator is a shortcut for declaring and⟦P156⟧initializing a variable in one line (Go uses the value on the right to⟦P157⟧determine the variable's type). Taking the long way, you might have⟦P158⟧written this as:⟦P159⟧Use the ⟦P160⟧ package's ⟦P161⟧⟦P162⟧⟦P163⟧ function⟦P164⟧ to create a greeting message. The⟦P165⟧first argument is a format string, and ⟦P166⟧ substitutes⟦P167⟧the ⟦P168⟧ parameter's value for the ⟦P169⟧ format⟦P170⟧verb. Inserting the value of the ⟦P171⟧ parameter completes⟦P172⟧the greeting text.⟦P173⟧Return the formatted greeting text to the caller.⟦P174⟧In the next step, you'll call this function from another module.⟦P175⟧⟦P176⟧Call your code from another module ⟦P177⟧⟦P178⟧⟦P179⟧
